Dysmenorrhoea
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Cyclic lower abdominal cramping, Pain radiating to back/thighs, Onset with menses

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-012
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe pelvic pain and fainting episodes associated with the onset of menses, requiring immediate clinical evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a young female experiencing extreme, spasmodic lower abdominal pain causing repeated presyncope (near-fainting) upon standing. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess hemodynamic stability, rule out acute blood loss or ectopic pregnancy, and provide immediate pain control to prevent a syncopal fall and injury before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The pain in my stomach and back is so sharp that every time I try to get up, the room spins and my legs give out.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began two hours ago, precisely onset with her menses. The patient reports excruciating cramping, spasmodic lower abdominal and pelvic pain that radiates sharply to the lower back and anterior thighs. She complains of generalized malaise, headache, and severe fatigue during the pain flares. The intense pain has caused two episodes of presyncope when she attempted to walk to the bathroom. She also notes increased bowel frequency today. This cyclic lower abdominal cramping is a predictable pattern repeating with each ovulatory cycle but is acutely severe tonight.
Functional Status: Confined to the floor/couch; unable to safely ambulate due to pain-induced presyncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of syncopal fall and head injury if ambulation is attempted without assistance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 mmHg (supine) | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient is lying supine on the couch, pale, diaphoretic, and groaning in pain.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals diffuse suprapubic tenderness to deep palpation, without rebound or rigidity. Bowel sounds are active. No palpable masses. Orthostatic vitals were attempted; patient became acutely pale and dizzy upon sitting up, so the test was aborted. Urine pregnancy test (performed on a sample collected with assistance) is negative.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways, roommate is present and highly supportive.
Medication Reconciliation: Took paracetamol 1g two hours ago with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Primary Dysmenorrhoea with pain-induced vasovagal presyncope - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out definitively by the negative point-of-care urine beta-hCG test.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Unlikely due to the absence of fever, prior normal vaginal discharge, and the strict cyclical nature of the severe pain.

Ovarian Cyst Rupture - Unlikely given the bilateral, crampy nature of the pain radiating to the thighs, rather than a sudden, sharp, unilateral peritoneal pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe cyclic lower abdominal cramping and pain radiating to the back and thighs, resulting in vasovagal presyncope and tachycardia. The objective clinical evidence, including a negative pregnancy test and diffuse, non-surgical abdominal tenderness, confirms severe primary dysmenorrhoea. The severity of the pain causing hemodynamic shifts (presyncope) and functional immobility necessitated urgent after-hours assessment. Failing to provide immediate parenteral analgesia and stabilize the patient's autonomic response would have exposed her to a high risk of syncopal injury and necessitated an emergency department transfer prior to the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular diclofenac 75mg for rapid, potent anti-inflammatory pain relief. Advised the patient to remain supine with legs elevated for the next hour to stabilize blood pressure. Prescribed naproxen 500mg twice daily with food for the remainder of the menses.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent dizziness when getting up, sit on the edge of the bed for a full 60 seconds before standing; experiencing a stable transition will reinforce the habit of slow postural changes during severe pain flares. To drastically reduce pain intensity, apply a continuous heat wrap to the lower back and abdomen; feeling the immediate soothing effect will encourage the use of localized physical therapies.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss a comprehensive transvaginal ultrasound to confirm a normal uterus and adnexa and rule out secondary causes.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call an ambulance (000) or present to the Emergency Department immediately if you actually faint and lose consciousness, or if the pain becomes sudden, sharp, and localized to one specific side of your abdomen.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive for primary dysmenorrhoea; a formal ultrasound is required to rule out structural issues.
Clinical Course: The intramuscular injection should provide profound relief within 45 minutes, allowing you to stand without feeling dizzy. The dizziness is a reflex from the severe pain, not from blood loss.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk unassisted until the pain has completely subsided. Seek emergency care if you faint.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The extreme pain from the uterine cramps triggered a nerve reflex that dropped your blood pressure, causing the dizzy spells. The injection will rapidly halt the cramping, which will, in turn, stop the dizzy spells and allow you to move safely.
Actionable Steps: Remain lying down for the next hour. Start taking the naproxen with a full meal once you are able to sit up comfortably.

